<title>PHILIPPINES: Manila's short-term PHIBOR rates decline.</title>
<headline>Manila's short-term PHIBOR rates decline.</headline>
<dateline>MANILA 1997-07-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>Short-term Philippine interbank offered rates (PHIBOR) declined as banks lent out more pesos to cover their dollar positions, traders said.</p>
<p>&quot;People were lending pesos to borrow dollars,&quot; a foreign bank dealer said, resulting in a downward adjustment in swap rates.</p>
<p>He said the market was experiencing the lag effect of the covering of dollar positions by banks for the end of the first six months.</p>
<p>&quot;Dollars abroad were more expensive at this time of the year,&quot; he said.  </p>
<p>Average rates quoted by 20 banks were:</p>
<p>			  JULY 1		 JUNE 30</p>
<p>    1 W		12  1/2		 12  5/8</p>
<p>    2 W		12  1/16		12  1/8</p>
<p>    1 M		11  7/16		11  5/8</p>
<p>    2 M		11  9/16		11  9/16</p>
<p>    3 M		11  9/16		11  9/16</p>
<p>    6 M		11  9/16		11  9/16</p>
<p>    12M		11  9/16		11  9/16</p>
<p>    VAL		2-Jul		   1-Jul</p>
<p>-- Lilian Karunungan (63 2) 841-8937</p>
